<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ey Iran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first performed 27 October <a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ای ایران, Ey Irân</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Ey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>&quot; was made during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In September 1941 Allied forces occupied Iran after the Anglo-Soviet invasion that overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a>. The poet Hossein Gol-e-Golab was moved to write a poem on Iranian nationalism when he saw Allied flags flying over an Iranian military barracks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>; by his own account, a confrontation between a British soldier and an Iranian officer encouraged him further.</p></div>
